                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_68\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit renumbers the `switch (oldVersion)` fall-through cases in\nKeychainDatabase.java's onUpgrade() method, converting a broken sequence\n(case 7 -> case 8 -> case 9 -> case 10 ...) into a correct, consecutive\nsequence (case 7 -> case 8 -> case 9 ...) by removing a spurious extra\ncase label. Specifically, the fix removes:\n\n    case 7:\n        // consolidate\n    case 8:\n        // new table for allowed key ids in API\n        ...\n\nand replaces it with just:\n\n    case 7:\n        // new table for allowed key ids in API\n        ...\n\nthen shifts every subsequent case number down by one (case 9->8, 10->9,\n11->10, 12->11, 13->12, 14->13, 15->14). This means at some point two\ncase labels (an empty \"case 7: // consolidate\" followed immediately by\n\"case 8: // new table...\") were introduced where there should only have\nbeen one, causing DATABASE_VERSION bumps to no longer line up correctly\nwith the case numbers in the upgrade switch statement further down (an\noff-by-one migration bug).\n\ncandidate_68 is exactly where this problematic pattern was first\nintroduced. It bumps DATABASE_VERSION from 7 to 8, adds the new\nCREATE_API_APPS_ALLOWED_KEYS table/columns, and inserts the migration\nlogic as:\n\n    case 7:\n        // consolidate\n    case 8:\n        // new table for allowed key ids in API\n        try {\n            db.execSQL(CREATE_API_APPS_ALLOWED_KEYS);\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            // never mind, the column probably already existed\n        }\n\nThis is the precise buggy code (extra/duplicate \"consolidate\" case\nlabel preceding the real migration case) that the fix commit later\nremoves and renumbers. No other candidate before or after touches these\ncase labels, confirming candidate_68 as the commit that introduced the\nmigration numbering bug.\n"
